/*
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
 * Copyright (C) 2026 The Fletcher Authors
 *
 * The shim's ELF export table, stated rather than inferred.
 *
 * ── Why visibility settings are not enough ──────────────────────────────────
 * `CXX_VISIBILITY_PRESET hidden` governs the symbols the compiler emits with
 * DEFAULT visibility from our own translation units, and `--exclude-libs ALL`
 * localises what arrives from a static archive. Between them they miss one set,
 * and BIND-2c found it on the first CI run that had any: libstdc++ marks a
 * handful of `shared_ptr` internals with EXPLICIT default visibility —
 *
 *     typeinfo and typeinfo-name for std::_Sp_counted_base<_S_atomic>
 *     typeinfo and typeinfo-name for std::_Mutex_base<_S_atomic>
 *     typeinfo-name for std::_Sp_make_shared_tag
 *     the function-local __tag in std::_Sp_make_shared_tag::_S_ti()
 *
 * — because RTTI comparison and control-block type identity have to work across
 * a shared-object boundary. An explicit attribute beats a -fvisibility default,
 * so a single `std::make_shared` in one of our own files puts all six in the
 * export table no matter what the target properties say. They appeared the
 * moment `fl_codec` started sharing its field plan with `fl_rows`.
 *
 * ── Why localising them is safe here ────────────────────────────────────────
 * Those symbols exist so that two shared objects can agree on a type. Nothing
 * crosses this boundary that would need such an agreement: `binding.h` names no
 * C++ type, no `shared_ptr` is handed out, and no exception escapes — the
 * containment site catches every one. The shim's `shared_ptr`s are born and die
 * inside it.
 *
 * ── What this file is ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The anonymous version below exports exactly the `fl_` prefix and localises
 * everything else, which makes "the shim exports only what binding.h declares"
 * a property of the LINK rather than a thing CI greps for afterwards. The grep
 * stays as the assertion that this file still works.
 *
 * Anonymous on purpose: a named version tag would put a version on every symbol
 * and a consumer resolving `fl_binding_abi_version` by plain name — which is
 * exactly what P/Invoke does — would have to know it.
 *
 * Windows needs none of this and gets none of it: nothing leaves a DLL without
 * `__declspec(dllexport)`. Its export-table problem is the opposite one and is a
 * BIND-9 question (see the CMakeLists comment).
 */
{
  global:
    fl_*;
  local:
    *;
};
